So I think there's a big misconception here, especially among self-published authors, that you need to start promoting your book six months, eight months, even a year ahead of time. That's simply not necessary. In fact, starting too early can backfire. And then I'll explain why later. But the reality is, most self-publishing platforms don't require any lead time. They allow you to upload your manuscript and your cover and publish immediately. There's no waiting period. So unless you're doing things like pre-orders, which we'll discuss separately, you can literally publish your book tomorrow. So the timing isn't dictated by the platform. It's dictated by your own preparation and readiness. And again, I'm not saying never promote early. What I am saying is that you don't have to wait until you've finished writing to start thinking about how you're going to market your book. You can absolutely begin planning and preparing. But you should not delay your actual publication date just to give yourself more time to market. That's the mistake a lot of people make.

