I've seen people try to send out manuscripts to reviewers months before publication. That's too early. It's like trying to put a firecracker into someone's hand while they're still holding onto a live grenade. I mean, they don't have time to process it. They're already overwhelmed with work. So you can't just throw something at them. You need to think strategically.

But then there's another extreme—waiting until the last minute. That doesn’t work either. Because now your window is closed. The media outlets aren't interested anymore. The momentum has passed. So you need to find this sweet spot. When should you reach out?

Well, here's my advice: aim for six weeks before the official release date. That gives reviewers enough time to read, digest, and respond—but not so much time that they forget about it. Six weeks is the Goldilocks zone.

Now, let me tell you why this matters. Early engagement isn't just about getting reviews—it's about building trust and credibility. When publishers see that you’ve been proactive and strategic, they take you seriously. They’re more likely to invest in you. So, even though it might feel awkward to reach out so far in advance, it’s actually a sign of professionalism.

And finally, remember: timing is everything. Don't wait too long. Don't go too early. Find that perfect moment—the sweet spot—and act decisively.

